{"id": "FC1-c147", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Which sum of stage deposits is S_n?", "back": "The sum of the deposits received at stages 0 to n, S_n = Σ_{t=0}^{n} D_t (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c150", "lesson": "L02", "front": "What is an instrument?", "back": "A claim with terms."}
{"id": "FC1-c152", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Under leakage (A2′), is the limit of total deposits at most d/ρ?", "back": "Yes. Under A2′ with 0 ≤ φ ≤ 1, A1 with 0 < ρ ≤ 1, and the no-withdrawal and no-repayment parts of A3, the limit d/(1 − φ(1 − ρ)) is at most d/ρ (Proposition 3.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c153", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Under leakage (A2′), when does the limit of total deposits equal d/ρ?", "back": "Exactly when φ = 1 or ρ = 1, for an initial deposit d > 0 as in Definition 2.1 (Proposition 3.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c014", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Do the partial sums of Lab 1, Part A equal the closed form for stages 0 to 10?", "back": "Yes. Lab 1, Part A prints True for the exact comparison of the partial sums with the closed form for stages 0 to 10 (Proposition 3.1; Lab 1, Part A).", "grade": "T", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (typed-in parameters, exact comparison)"}
{"id": "FC1-c122", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Under Assumption 4.0, when is equity after a loss λ nonnegative?", "back": "Exactly when λ ≤ E, the equity before the loss (Proposition 4.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c030", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Does the register contain every combination of form and venue?", "back": "Yes, on a hand reading of register entries 1 (cash, exchange), 3 (derivative, exchange), 4 (cash, OTC) and 6 (derivative, OTC) of the Lab 2 code. Lab 2, Part C prints only the exchange entries by form, not the four combinations, so the claim rests on that hand reading (Example 3.1).", "grade": "C", "basis": "a hand reading of register entries 1 (cash, exchange), 3 (derivative, exchange), 4 (cash, OTC) and 6 (derivative, OTC) in the lab code; not printed by Lab 2"}
{"id": "FC1-c139", "lesson": "L03", "front": "In Example 2.5, what is the mid-move term of Proposition 2.3?", "back": "−1000 cents: the first trade, a 100-share customer sale at mid 10000 with terminal mid 9990, adds (−1)(100)(10000 − 9990) = −1000. The second trade is at the terminal mid and adds zero (Example 2.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c142", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When does the offsets_nondecreasing line of Lab 1 print False?", "back": "When the clearing offset is larger than the settlement lag (Section 1; Lab 1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c143", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a resting order?", "back": "The unfilled part of a limit order, waiting in the book at its price (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c151", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Does the mark-to-mid decomposition need the sequence to be closed?", "back": "No. Under A1 to A3 it holds for any finite sequence of trades, closed or not (Proposition 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
